From 23b110561dcaffcbe66e05aa4feeda8ab66e6ff1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: burntcarrot <aadhav.n1@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Mar 2026 12:06:18 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 06/11] sentry/kernel: serialized DST state snapshots
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Replaces the sfork-based CoW snapshot approach with a serialized state
model that works within the sentry's seccomp constraints.

The sfork approach called fork(2) from within the sentry (a multi-
threaded Go process). This caused two problems:
  1. The sentry's seccomp filter blocks SYS_FORK, triggering SIGSYS.
  2. Even if fork were allowed, the child Go runtime is in an
     inconsistent state (no OS threads for any goroutine except the
     caller) — the restored sentry would be non-functional.

The new approach:
  - Snapshot = serialize DST metadata {virtual clock NS, RNG seed,
    scheduler state, coverage bitmap snapshot} to an in-memory buffer.
  - Restore = deserialize metadata; the control plane is responsible
    for restarting the container and replaying with the saved seed/time.
  - The sentry's SnapshotTree stores snapshots in memory (no fork).
  - SnapshotTree.Restore() sends back the saved metadata so the control
    plane can apply it to a fresh container.

For deterministic containers this is semantically equivalent: the same
seed + same virtual time epoch → same execution. The control plane
(gvisor.go) handles container restart on restore.
